sentences.t:
Grande tuerto, le han tenido, sabémoslo todos nós;
alcaldes sean desto co[m]de don Anrric e co[m]de don Remond
e estos otros co[m]des que del vando non sodes.

sentences.l:
Great wrong, to-him they-have held, we-know-it all we;
judges let-be of-this count don Anrric and count don Remond
and these other counts who of-the faction not you-are.

sentences.t:
Esto les demando a ifantes de Carrión:
por mis fijas quem dexaron yo non he desonor,
ca vos las casastes, rey, sabredes qué fer oy;

sentences.l:
This them I-demand to infantes of Carrión:
for my daughters that-on-me they-abandoned I not have dishonour,
for you them married, king, you-will-know what to-do today;

sentences.t:
diles dos espadas a Colada e a Tizón
—estas yo las gané a guisa de varón,—
ques ondrassen con ellas e sirviessen a vos;

sentences.l:
I-gave-them two swords to Colada and to Tizón
—these I them I-won in manner of man,—
that-themselves they-should-honour with them and should-serve to you;

sentences.t:
quando dexaron mis fijas en el robredo de Corpes,
comigo non quisieron aver nada e perdieron mi amor;
denme mis espadas quando mios yernos non son.”

sentences.l:
when they-abandoned my daughters in the oak-wood of Corpes,
with-me not they-wished to-have anything and they-lost my favour;
let-them-give-me my swords since my sons-in-law not they-are.”

sentences.t:
Essora salién aparte ifantes de Carrión,
con todos s[o]s parientes y el bando que í son;
apriessa lo ivan trayendo e acuerdan la razón:

sentences.l:
Then were-going-out apart infantes of Carrión,
with all their kinsmen and the faction that there are;
quickly it they-were-treating and they-agree the answer:

sentences.t:
“Aun grand amor nos faze el Çid Campeador,
quando desondra de sus fijas no nos demanda oy;
bien nos abendremos con el rey don Alfons.

sentences.l:
“Still great favour to-us does the Çid Campeador,
when dishonour of his daughters not to-us he-demands today;
well ourselves we-shall-come-to-terms with the king don Alfons.

sentences.t:
Démosle sus espadas, quando assí finca la boz,
e quando las toviere, partir se a la cort;
ya mas non avrá derecho de nos el Çid Canpeador.”

sentences.l:
Let-us-give-him his swords, since thus ends the claim,
and when them he-should-have, break-up will the court;
now more not will-have right from us the Çid Canpeador.”

sentences.t:
Con aquesta fabla tornaron a la cort;
“Merçed, ya rey don Alfons, sodes nuestro señor!
No lo podemos negar, ca dos espadas nos dió;

sentences.l:
With this talk they-returned to the court;
“Mercy, O king don Alfons, you-are our lord!
Not it we-can deny, for two swords to-us he-gave;

sentences.t:
saca[n] las espadas e relumbra toda la cort,
las maçanas e los arriazes todos d' oro son;
maravíllanse dellas los omnes buenos de la cort.

sentences.l:
they-draw the swords and gleams all the court,
the pommels and the cross-guards all of gold are;
marvel at-them the men good of the court.

sentences.t:
A mio Çid llamó el rey las espadas le dio;
reçibió las espadas las manos le besó,
tornos al escaño don[t] se levantó.

sentences.l:
To my Çid called the king the swords to-him he-gave;
he-received the swords the hands to-him he-kissed,
turned-himself to-the bench whence himself he-raised.

sentences.t:
A so sobrino [don Pero] por nómbrel llamó,
tendió el braço, la espada Tizón le dió;
“Prendetla, sobrino, ca mejora en señor.”

sentences.l:
To his nephew [don Pero] by name-him he-called,
he-stretched the arm, the sword Tizón to-him he-gave;
“Take-it, nephew, for it-betters in lord.”
